#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557369} :activity/id #uuid "99747c81-e556-573e-9a49-314528507d7a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957 :topic/id #uuid "21390ce8-05ac-5fcf-ba45-89cb6f33aadc" :topic/math-academy-id 3147} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aec11462-7527-5c1f-bee1-5311de75df3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163877} {:answer/id #uuid "28a5d564-3fad-5bcd-bbd4-8313b0c26770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163878} {:answer/id #uuid "41a558e9-c044-57fc-99da-3a88324c2b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163879} {:answer/id #uuid "5336652c-5f4b-557f-994b-7062409121b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163880} {:answer/id #uuid "667f6b41-1da3-5dde-85af-5f6ba8248df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163880} :answer-field/id #uuid "91ed5a91-8a47-5da8-b4fa-8f64aaacc1c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a40ddcf2-e6e2-5966-908e-b51c9f5915b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163060" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+3y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}+3xy]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}18{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[6{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 48 & =(2a+3\\cdot 1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 8 & =2a+3 \\\\ 5 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{5}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{5}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2fdac4bf-88bd-5b8c-8156-abdd95028924" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305e3d19-c9fb-5ff4-8bea-3ba01ec37d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279015} {:answer/id #uuid "553dbfe6-1300-5f62-9d6e-2745efdb74e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279369} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca44dc-1a03-569e-8017-76f798ba4acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279872} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7bea68-5a2c-568c-9b79-3781c74a681b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280257} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbef370-76d9-5b05-9bcd-73012f686620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280797} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ca4de9-3198-5afe-9619-0e002b132fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "185b68b4-cdef-58dd-9006-867ebfafd06d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163059" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y+1,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}+y]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+12x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+12\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =f(1,b)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =({1}^{2}+2b+1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =12b+12 \\\\ 20 & =12b \\\\ b & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,\\frac{5}{3})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{5}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "76a6bdfb-b2d3-5c1f-94fe-835169048009" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a00aa3-d6d0-54f6-84c3-a84dbc1e7955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556539} {:answer/id #uuid "9affac74-7d2b-5cbc-a27c-c731e04fc635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556805} {:answer/id #uuid "b26cb4c0-36e4-5059-b498-2f505edf4153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556869} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f175b1-b2d8-5ccc-8542-dafe46581974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556878} {:answer/id #uuid "e442d8f5-617c-59ab-a4ac-822779b6d576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556878} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dce9295-adc4-54f0-85bb-a7daa031279d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6236dff-5836-5e2b-be74-a41334b247e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163156" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 4x\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}6xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[12{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =(6a\\cdot 2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "837149fd-427b-5257-8438-799b8410b152" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557383}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557383 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b5a5360-ed9f-5a7e-9e52-047728bc73d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279432} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee39258-8e4f-57f7-a350-4850b92f4855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280004} {:answer/id #uuid "b98bfa68-5b2a-5844-b047-3d433642ead1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280224} {:answer/id #uuid "d87db86d-f745-5889-b660-e63f2c4d38e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280504} {:answer/id #uuid "f5377fd4-0439-592f-84f9-3e1c47801136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280504} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b2d4cbb-97db-5842-8cbf-0379ee6eb626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6b96aef-d640-5c31-9a3e-e83b8daeccf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163206" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+2y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a+2\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 4 & =2a+4 \\\\ 0 & =2a \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(0,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "99580cac-da4a-5eeb-8345-69ac73a2ebdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e3253af-36cf-50a6-b6a4-e0cebd8a088c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279300} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb31ddb-172c-5405-bfcb-0893d02422b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279460} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d136b5-8a1b-538d-932f-235fc197906b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280044} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c2097d-3ba9-5baa-934e-6dd265730b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280216} {:answer/id #uuid "c944cd9a-1153-596c-ae76-dc3d60055c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279460} :answer-field/id #uuid "87d2b3a3-7b86-546b-8f02-ed9d3ed11cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33038417-f976-5b04-ba1c-849f23418261" :question/math-academy-id "q-163207" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x+y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x+\\frac{9}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9x}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\cdot (3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9\\cdot (3)}{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27 & =f(2,b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =(2+b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =9b+18 \\\\ 9 & =9b \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "c19cb2ee-7084-5301-85f3-3815cc4bddab"}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556415}]}
